  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1000 PM MDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nISOLATED SHOWERS AND STORMS FRIDAY AND SATURDAY:\n\nA weak jet accompanied by some modest moisture will nose into \nthe Four Corners region tonight. Some of the hi-res models \nsupport the development of showers along the moisture transport \ngradient, especially in southern foothills of the San Juans. \nThis moisture remains in place for much of Friday and Saturday, \nespecially along the Divide. As a result, afternoon convection \nfavoring the higher terrain will return to the forecast, but \nmost locales will remain dry with seasonable temperatures and \nbreezy afternoon winds.\n\nMONSOONAL MOISTURE SURGES SUNDAY AND MONDAY: \n\nWith southwesterly flow remaining in place through the holiday \nweekend, moisture advection will continue to intensify \nthroughout the holiday weekend. This will result in scattered to\nwidespread convection across the region. PWATs upwards of 200% \nof normal will support the potential for heavy rainfall and \nflash flooding, especially around recent burn scars and areas \nwith steep terrain. This threat is particularly heightened where\nsoils have reached peak saturation after multiple rounds of \nstorms this past week. \n\nBeyond Monday, model outcomes begin to diverge from each other.\nThe GFS appears to be heavy handed bringing in a second \ntropical system over the Baja Peninsula late next week, which \nmost other models do not have a strong signal on. So at this \ntime, there is uncertainty with how long we hold onto moisture \nchances next week and therefore forecast confidence remains low \nbeyond Monday.",
